{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790320096,"nonce":"A+Q2JgKicfZ4++5E42RSpxlhqcA=","message":"Round 3 (branch w2/L-gc-semantics rebased onto main 4771a187; fixes 1690aced, 004bdae0, e42167ff, 54c6a30f).\n\nWhy the two cases failed in Mac debug builds: the word that kept the \"phoenix\" object (and the weak-value key in weak_value_keys) was the `addr` temporary of the interpreter's Op::Gep arm, in the native frame of HirInterpreter::run_frame. A run_frame that had returned (the one that built the object) left it there, and the run_frame of zl_collectgarbage, made at the same depth, never writes that slot. Debug frames are about 48 KB, release 0x768 bytes, so only debug builds kept the word. Scrubbing below host_gc could not reach it, because the frame is live during the collection. Two more instances turned up once that was fixed: a stale word in a live call_by_id frame (a returned sibling call at the same depth), and, with warm-up on, words that compiled callees left below the interpreter's frames, which a later run_frame laid over. Found with a new collector report: ZYNTAX_GC_EXPLAIN=1 records which word first reached each block, and Marking::why names the chain, a stack word by the function whose frame holds it (frame-pointer walk plus dladdr). The Lua host prints it for finalizable objects and keys of nil values that survive the program's own collectgarbage.\n\nFix (004bdae0): once weak hooks are registered, the interpreter notes how deep its frames and native calls reach and, after each call op returns, zeroes the stack below the calling frame down to that depth. Each run_frame also starts on cleared stack, sized by the largest run_frame frame measured so far, which covers what compiled callees left. Clearing is bounded to the thread's own stack range, so a fiber stack is never touched. Programs without weak tables or finalizers only read one relaxed flag per interpreted call.\n\nFound while verifying (e42167ff): the lowered pairs loop finds the next live position at the end of an iteration and reads the value at the start of the next. An OSR transfer at the loop head allocates, and a collection there can clear the weak value in between, so the loop yielded (key, nil). The verifier's e7 program hit it under ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1 once finalizer-nested collections stopped trimming. A nil value read at a found position now moves on to the next live position. Case gc/weak_list_in_finalizer.lua (gc_stressed exposes it; the build before this fix printed bad 1).\n\n54c6a30f: the weak sweeper consumes State.explicit, so a collection that an allocation in a finalizer starts (finalizers run inside collect, after the world runs again) is automatic and trims nothing.\n\nNew case gc/finalize_after_return.lua: objects built and handled by returned calls are finalized by the next collection, whether it is called at that depth or deeper. The gc category now has 15 cases. The count of 14 in the round-2 report was wrong: that round had 13.\n\nVerified: gc, warm_up::gc and gc_stressed pass in Mac debug (default features, and llvm-backend), Mac release (llvm-backend), NUC release and NUC debug (llvm-backend). Loops of all 15 cases x 3 modes: Mac 765 runs across debug, debug llvm-backend (tiered and ZYLUA_LLVM=1) and release; NUC release 360 runs, NUC debug 225 runs; 0 failures. Before the run_frame pre-clear, weak_value_keys with warm-up on failed 9 of 60 in a Mac debug llvm-backend build; after it, 0 of 60.\n\nUnchanged: the verifier's breaking programs give the same SAME/DIFF table as the build before this round (24 same, 16 differ). The remaining differences are the conservative-stack survivors in release builds (e2b, e2c, v1b keep 1 key), coroutine rooting (224cd147eb57b76b29c83e088dcf50fcc0481481b9f2fb16561622d3751f1166), and timing. Official gc.lua stops at 201 (step is a whole cycle), gengc.lua at 122, closure.lua at 253.\n\nPerf note: in interleaved A/B on the NUC (load 2.5 to 6), method_call's kernel elapsed reads 1.04 to 1.15 against main 4771a187 for both the package before this round and after it (wall 0.98 to 1.04), so it comes from the earlier package commits, not from this round. The tiering sequence is identical. Other kernels are within the noise.","files":null}]}